          "text": "{\"evidence\":[{\"paragraph_id\":\"p3\",\"quote\":\"Many trans-Neptunian objects (TNOs) have been discovered; in many cases their positions in this list are approximate, as there is frequently a large uncertainty in their estimated diameters due to their distance from Earth.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p6\",\"quote\":\"There can be difficulty in determining the diameter (within a factor of about 2) for typical objects beyond Saturn (see: 2060 Chiron § Physical characteristics , for an example) .\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p14\",\"quote\":\"For simplicity and comparative purposes, the values are manually calculated assuming that the bodies are all spheres.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p16\",\"quote\":\"Many intrinsically bright TNOs like 2018 VG 18 and 2017 OF 201 do not have directly measured sizes (e.g. via stellar occultation and radiometry of thermal emission ), so their sizes are estimated based on an assumed albedo .\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p31\",\"quote\":\"^ a b Radius has been determined by various methods, such as optical ( Hubble ), thermal ( Spitzer ), or direct imaging via spacecraft\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p44\",\"quote\":\"^ a b c d e f g h i j k Radius estimated from an assumed albedo\"}]}"
